# Without this file the build context is ~50 GB — `target/` alone is 45 — and
# every `docker build` spends minutes uploading files no image stage reads.

# Compiled Rust. The builder stage compiles from source inside the image, so
# a host `target/` is not just useless but actively wrong: it is macOS
# object code being shipped to a Linux builder.
target/

# Node installs, at every depth. The `**/` matters: .dockerignore patterns are
# matched against the whole relative path and do NOT recurse on their own, so
# a bare `node_modules/` would exclude only the root one and let
# `apps/web/node_modules` (165 MB of macOS-native binaries) into the context —
# where `COPY apps/web/ apps/web/` would then bury the Linux install the
# builder just made.
**/node_modules

# The Expo client. None of the three server images build from it, and it
# carries ~3 GB of Android build output. `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`
# still succeeds without it — the lockfile lists it as an importer, but a
# filtered install never visits that importer.
apps/mobile/

# Build output — regenerated inside the image.
**/dist

# Local state. `.env` holds the real JWT_SECRET and MEDIA_TOKEN_SECRET; a
# COPY that swept it into a layer would publish both to anyone who pulls
# the image.
.env
.env.*
**/*.log
**/.DS_Store

# Not read by any build stage.
.git/
.github/
.claude/
docs/
README.md
Makefile
docker-compose.yml

# `deploy/` is NOT excluded: Dockerfile.web copies deploy/nginx.conf into the
# runtime stage. Only the compose files and docs under it are dead weight.
deploy/README.md
deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml
deploy/env.prod.example
